WEBVTT

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.415
CTI collection is the process of gathering information that may answer an intelligence

00:00:04.415 --> 00:00:05.390
requirement.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:09.185
Useful sources include internal telemetry, incident reports, malware reports,

00:00:09.185 --> 00:00:12.635
vulnerability advisories, vendor reporting, government advisories,

00:00:12.635 --> 00:00:16.770
ISAC or community sharing, open-source reporting, and threat research blogs.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.520
Internal sources are especially valuable because they show what is happening in the

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.610
organization's own environment.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.815
External sources add broader context, but they must be evaluated before they drive action.

00:00:25.815 --> 00:00:27.745
More sources are not automatically better.

00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:31.900
Each source has reliability, timeliness, duplication, licensing, and bias issues.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.755
A vendor report may be high quality but focused on customers of that vendor.

00:00:35.755 --> 00:00:39.211
A public blog may contain useful technical findings but omit uncertainty.

00:00:39.211 --> 00:00:42.120
A community share may be timely but incomplete.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.630
A feed may include indicators that are old, reused, or unrelated to the organization.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.975
Source value depends on the requirement and the decision being supported.

00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:52.975
Collection discipline means asking what the source can answer and what it cannot.

00:00:52.975 --> 00:00:56.350
If the requirement is about exploitation of a specific vulnerability,

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.825
the analyst may need vulnerability advisories,

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:02.575
exploit observations from trusted reporting, internal exposure data,

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:04.330
and incident telemetry.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.755
If the requirement is about phishing targeting employees,

00:01:06.755 --> 00:01:11.030
the analyst may need email security logs, reported messages, domain registration context,

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.795
and relevant campaign reporting.

00:01:12.795 --> 00:01:14.705
The source set changes with the question.

00:01:14.705 --> 00:01:17.165
Source reliability should be recorded in plain language.

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:20.660
An analyst might note that a source is firsthand, secondhand, automated,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.559
community-submitted, vendor-curated, or internally observed.

00:01:23.559 --> 00:01:26.195
They might also note whether the source usually reports quickly,

00:01:26.195 --> 00:01:30.105
whether it corrects mistakes, and whether it separates evidence from interpretation.

00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:32.915
This does not require a complicated scoring system at first.

00:01:32.915 --> 00:01:36.560
It does require enough discipline that another analyst can understand why the source was

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.655
trusted or treated cautiously.

00:01:38.655 --> 00:01:40.800
Processing and handling rules begin during collection.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.775
Some sources have licensing restrictions.

00:01:42.775 --> 00:01:45.625
Some shared intelligence has Traffic Light Protocol handling.

00:01:45.625 --> 00:01:48.610
Some internal data contains personal information, customer data,

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.736
or sensitive investigation details.

00:01:50.736 --> 00:01:54.080
Speed does not remove the obligation to protect sources, respect privacy,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.779
and follow sharing rules.

00:01:55.779 --> 00:01:59.940
A good CTI process tracks where information came from and how it may be used.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.775
Collection must stay defensive and authorized.

00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:05.620
This course does not teach unauthorized access, intrusive collection, evasion,

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.355
or offensive procedures.

00:02:07.355 --> 00:02:12.070
Defensive CTI teams can do strong work using approved telemetry, incident evidence,

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:14.755
trusted reporting, community sharing, and open sources.

00:02:14.755 --> 00:02:17.965
The quality of collection is not measured by how much data is gathered.

00:02:17.965 --> 00:02:21.350
It is measured by whether the collected material can be trusted, handled correctly,

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.130
and turned into useful analysis.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.910
A practical collection plan should be small enough to maintain.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.405
List the requirement, the sources selected, the reason each source was selected,

00:02:29.405 --> 00:02:31.720
the handling limits, and the refresh rhythm.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.690
If a source never changes a decision, remove it or lower its priority.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.420
Collection discipline is also backlog discipline.
